{
  "diagnosis": "The incumbent fused kernel (attempt 2) correctly fuses mean, variance, normalization, and affine transform for irregular shape with masking and zero-padding. It compiles and runs successfully, achieving a ~1.88x speedup over the baseline two-kernel solution without spilling or hardware bottlenecks. Attempts to improve throughput by pipelining (attempt 3) or unrolled reductions (attempts 4 and 5) failed due to unsupported Triton primitives (tl.next_stage), unsupported tensor slicing, and non-constexpr arguments to tl.arange, respectively. These API misuse errors prevent compilation and no runtime data is available for these variants. The single-stage, 4-warp fused kernel remains the best validated implementation.",
  "outcome": "The best performing and validated kernel fuses all layernorm steps with masking and zero-padding, uses a fixed RB=1024 block size, and launches one program per row (grid=513). It delivers nearly 2x speedup over the baseline with no numerical correctness issues and no hardware resource bottlenecks detected. More aggressive performance tuning attempts failed due to Triton API constraints.",
  "next_step": "Adopt the incumbent fused kernel from attempt 2 as the production solution. To improve performance further, explore tuning warp counts (4 to 8 warps) and pipeline stages (1 to 2) only if fully supported by Triton. Implement any unrolling or pipelining using only supported Triton features, ensuring all tl.arange calls use compile-time constants and masking is correctly applied. Avoid unsupported primitives like tl.next_stage and unsupported tensor slicing. Profile on multiple shapes to confirm scaling and masking overhead. Optionally validate mixed precision or alternative reduction schemes for incremental gains."
}
